  "passage": "A town passed a rule limiting garden watering during dry months. Some residents were angry because they loved green lawns.\nThe town explained that drinking water, farms, and fire safety had to come first. It also offered workshops on plants that need less water.\nAt first, the town looked less green. Later, new gardens appeared with stones, local plants, and shaded soil. Many were beautiful in a different way.\nResidents learned that adapting does not always mean losing beauty. It can mean changing the idea of beauty to fit real conditions.\nA water rule became a design lesson. Scarcity can force creativity.",
